#import "common.typ": *
#align(center)[
  #text(1.25em)[*T_After notes*]
]

#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 12pt)
  Figuring out what the Value of `T_After` actually represents
]

#p(1)[
  After figuring out what the T_After I collected actually represents, I found I was misguided previously about what it represents.

  The code was:

  #figure(
    [
      ```cpp

      Float pr = T_maj[0] * mp.sigma_s[0];
      beta *= T_maj * mp.sigma_s / pr;
      r_u *= T_maj * mp.sigma_s / pr;

      ```
    ],
    caption: [Spectral MIS sampling],
  )<spectral_MIS>
  And then later...
  ```cpp
  if (!scattered && beta)
  {
      beta *= T_maj / T_maj[0];
      r_u *= T_maj / T_maj[0];
      r_l *= T_maj / T_maj[0];
  }
  ```

  And the calculation I performed at the end is:

  $
    T_"ray segment" = beta_"after" / beta_"before"
  $

  So what I thought was the transmission just for the ray segment that was sampled, it was actually something different. Due to the extra `/ pr`, it actually represented a sort of Weighted Throughput based on Importance Sampling.

  So instead what I calculated was:
  $
    T_"MIS Weighted" = beta_"after" / beta_"before"
  $

]

#heading(depth: 1)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  Throughput Ratio vs Pure Transmittance
]


#p(1)[
  #show link: underline
  #show link: highlight

  Initially, I thought it predicted just a Pure transmittance, meaning it is the fraction of radiance that exits the medium from the radiance that enters. So a transmittance of 0.5 means only half the radiance that entered exits. This is also per wavelength (or per r, g, b) so some mediums would let more red light through than say blue or green.

  However, from the calculation above, it actually calculates a *weighted throughput ratio* rather than pure transmittance. This ratio includes:

  1. *Physical transmittance*: $e^(-sigma dot d) in [0,1]$
  2. *Importance sampling corrections*: wavelength-dependent weights that
    can exceed 1.0 to maintain unbiased estimation
  3. *RGB color space conversion*: spectral resampling artifacts

  This can be seen from the visualization of the data:
  #image("t_after_graph.png")

  From an analysis of training data from a subset of 75,000 samples, we can see that there are many samples in which the weighted ratio is higher than 1, up to 1.6, particularly in the
  blue channel. This is expected for pbrt's ratio tracking integrator, where
  importance sampling weights correct for sampling bias.


  However, PBRT only picks a single wavelength to perform to compute PDF to sample from that decides the type of scattering:

  ```cpp
  // Compute probabilities for each type of scattering.
  Float pAbsorb = mp.sigma_a[0] / sigma_maj[0];
  Float pScatter = mp.sigma_s[0] / sigma_maj[0];
  Float pNull = std::max<Float>(0, 1 - pAbsorb - pScatter);
  PBRT_DBG("Medium scattering probabilities: %f %f %f\n", pAbsorb,
      pScatter, pNull);

  // And randomly choose one.
  int mode = SampleDiscrete({ pAbsorb, pScatter, pNull }, uMode);
  ```

  However, this would become *biased* towards the PDF of the first wavelength considered in SampledWavelengths (say 450nm). But we want to produce an unbiased estimator (to ensure energy is not created/destroyed) and would want on average for it to be unbiased/correct. Therefore, later in @spectral_MIS it divides by `pr`, which can lead to situations where the the spectral MIS portion of `T_maj * mp.sigma_s / pr` is greater than 1 if for example it is very uncommon for the first wavelength to scatter but common for longer wavelengths (like red) to scatter. Because the event `pr` for the first wavelength is uncommon it will boost this ray's contribution to ensure it is unbiased/to compensate.

  All of this is explained in #link("https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Light_Transport_II_Volume_Rendering/Volume_Scattering_Integrators#x2-ChromaticMedia")[Chromatic Media] in the PBRT Book


  I then perform a `ToOutputRGB` call which then converts these into RGB signals instead of the (typically 4) wavelengths according to the colourspace which by default is the CIE xyz colorspace. This boosts blue wavelengths even more helping to cause the 1.6 Transmission value as shown in the histogram above.
]



