#p(2)[
  They look very similar but the key thing it learned is that blue does not have a transmittance of 0/opacity of 1 in empty space, but instead it correctly learns where it would be transmitted and where it would not

  The results don't seem to effect radiance values at all and they remain largely similar to the ones for the clamped values. This makes sense, only the transmittance/weighted throughput ratio was affected so radiance should remain unaffected
]


#heading(depth: 1)[
  Evaluation
]

#p(1)[
  Overall, although the radiance remains unaffected, due to the increase in PSNR as well as the improvement of
]


#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  What this means for the model
]

#p(2)[
  From the results shown above, I decided its best to pursue linear scaling (dividing by max=1.6) for the final model/for inference rather than clamping, preserving the full distribution and also preserving the overestimates/compensation caused by the spectral MIS in PBRT. This is required to ensure an unbiased estimator in the final output image. This does require adding a new member variable `m_volume_training_tAfter_scale` to ensure the model can rescale for inference.
]

#heading(depth: 2)[
  #set text(size: 11pt)
  How this changes Inference
]

#p(2)[
  It doesn't really change the post calculations from the network outputs during inference:
  ```cpp
  Transmittance = network(p, d, tMax)
  beta *= Transmittance
  ```

  and all it would be now is

  ```cpp
  weighted_T = network(p, r, tMax)

  beta *= weighted_T
  ```
]
